,title,link,text,language,family
0,Parakanã people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parakanã_people,"November 8, 2025 - The Parakanã are known for their elaborate music and dance rituals as well. Four of these routines are the most well-known, as they require an intense amount of preparation and development. They are called the Clarinet Feast, the Cigar Feast, the Feast of the Rhythmic Baton, and the Beer Feast.",Parakanã,Tupian
1,Tupi–Guarani languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tupi–Guarani_languages,"2 weeks ago - Tenetehara (Group IV): Akwáwa (dialects: Asuriní, Suruí do Pará, Parakanã), Avá-Canoeiro, Tapirapé, Tenetehára (dialects: Guajajara, Tembé), Turiwára",Parakanã,Tupian
2,Tupian languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tupian_languages,"April 24, 2026 - The Tupi or Tupian language family comprises some 70 languages spoken in South America, of which the best known are Tupi proper and Guarani. Rodrigues (2007) considers the Proto-Tupian urheimat to be somewhere between the Guaporé and Aripuanã rivers, in the Madeira River basin.",Parakanã,Tupian
3,"Instruments of Power: Musicalising the Other in Lowland South America: Ethnomusicology Forum: Vol 22, No 3",https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/17411912.2013.844440,"4 The Wakuénai also have a highly elaborate repertoire of ritual music played on sacred wind instruments that are regarded as parts of the body of the primordial human being of myth and that participate directly in shamanic powers of mythic creation and destruction (see Hill Citation1993, Citation2009b). 5 See Fausto (Citation2012) for a nuanced argument about the meanings of friendship among the Tupian Parakanã of Brazil.",Parakanã,Tupian
4,Parakanã - Indigenous Peoples in Brazil,https://pib.socioambiental.org/en/Povo:Parakanã,"At the same time, young boys, less than eight years old, are also encouraged to participate as musicians: “so that they grow, it is said” (tojemotowi oja). It is important, at this stage, to note a difference between the Western and the Eastern Parakanã, since, for the former, the feast ...",Parakanã,Tupian
5,"Parakanã Language (PAK) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map, Endangered Level & Official Use | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/pak/,"Parakanã is a stable indigenous language of Brazil. It belongs to the Tupian language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by all in the ethnic community. It is not known to be taught in schools.",Parakanã,Tupian
6,Endangered Languages Project - Parakanã,https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/2959,"[aka Parakanân, Parocana, Awaeté] Classification: Tupian · · endangered · Compare Sources (5) Information from: “Povos Indígenas no Brasil” . Ricardo, Fany Pantaleoni and Bruno Belivaqua Aguilar · · Information from: “Povos Indígenas no Brasil” .",Parakanã,Tupian
7,Parakana Language and the Parakanã Indian Tribe (Awaete),http://www.native-languages.org/parakana.htm,Language: Parakana is a Tupian language of South America. It is an agglutinative language.,Parakanã,Tupian
